Ethernet: collection of network protocols/standards

UTP cables use 4 twisted pairs (8 pins). For 10/100 Mbps Ethernet, pins 1–2 and 3–6 are the two pairs used for communication, with one pair transmitting and the other 
receiving

Full-duplex means sending and receiving simultaneously.

Traditionally, PCs, routers, and firewalls use the same pin assignment (1–2 = TX, 3–6 = RX), while switches use the opposite (1–2 = RX, 3–6 = TX). Therefore, a
straight-through cable is used between different interface types (e.g., PC → switch), while a crossover cable swaps 1–2 ↔ 3–6 and is traditionally used between the
same interface types (e.g., PC → PC, PC → router, router → router, firewall → router). With 1000BASE-T (Gigabit Ethernet) and 10GBASE-T, all 4 pairs are used, and each pair is
bidirectional, meaning every pair can simultaneously carry data in both directions; therefore, the old idea of specific pairs being exclusively TX or RX no longer applies.

Auto MDI-X, found on most modern Ethernet devices, automatically detects the TX/RX configuration and electronically adjusts it, meaning either a straight-through or
crossover cable can usually be used, eliminating the need to manually choose the correct cable.

Fiber optic cable: 2 connector in each end, one sends, the other receives (send connect to receive on one end and receive connect to send on the other). Singlemode fiber vs 
multimode fiber: multimode fiber allows for different angles of light waves to enter which allows the transmission of more data, whereas singlemode allows only one light wave.

Review:  utp -> low cost, shorter maximum distance (~100m), vulnerable to EMI, emit a faint signal which can be copied (security risk).
         multimode fiber optic -> average cost, average maximum distance (~500m), not vulnerable to EMI, does not emit signal.
         singlemode fiber optic -> high cost, higher maximum distance (~30km), not vulnerable to EMI, does not emit signal.
